<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Protest and freedom · 1911 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">The Preacher and the Slave</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>Joe Hill's 1911 union song, sung to a hymn tune, which gave the English language the phrase &quot;pie in the sky&quot;.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Joe Hill</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>tune of the hymn &quot;In the Sweet By-and-By&quot;</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Industrial Workers of the World (the Wobblies)</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>United States</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1911</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Protest and freedom</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Joe Hill, first published 1911 in the Little Red Songbook)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-preacher-and-the-slave">The Preacher and the Slave</a>&quot; was written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joe-hill">Joe Hill</a> in 1911 as a parody of the Christian hymn &quot;In the Sweet By-and-By&quot;. Borrowing the hymn and its musical style was also a way to take over the feeling such music carried and turn it to a purpose that was not religious. The song was first titled &quot;Long Haired Preachers&quot; and is often called &quot;Pie in the Sky&quot;; it was first published in the fourth edition of the Little Red Songbook in 1911.</p><p>The Industrial Workers of the World (IWW), commonly known as the Wobblies, spent much of its effort organising migrant workers in the lumber and construction camps. When the workers came back to the cities, the Wobblies met the Salvation Army, which they mocked as the &quot;Starvation Army&quot; and which was said to have tried to drown out the IWW with its religious music. Hill had first met the Salvation Army as a child in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. Several songs parodied the Salvation Army's hymns, and &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-preacher-and-the-slave">The Preacher and the Slave</a>&quot; was the most successful of them; in it Hill coined the phrase &quot;pie in the sky&quot;.</p><p>Harry McClintock is credited as the first to sing it in public, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woody-guthrie">Woody Guthrie</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pete-seeger">Pete Seeger</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a> Phillips sang it later. The novelist Wallace Stegner used its title for a novel based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joe-hill">Joe Hill</a>'s life.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A satire on the preachers and the Salvation Army bands who, the song says, answer hungry working men with promises of food in heaven &quot;bye and bye&quot; while they collect their coins, and who call a man a sinner for fighting to get something good for his wife and children in this life. In the last verses it turns to the workers of all countries, called to unite and fight for freedom, and throws the hymn's promise back at the &quot;grafters&quot;. The mood is mocking and comic, sung to a gentle hymn tune.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Long-haired preachers come out every night,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Try to tell you what's wrong and what's right;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But when asked how 'bout something to eat</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>They will answer with voices so sweet:</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>You will eat, bye and bye,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>In that glorious land above the sky;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Work and pray, live on hay,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>You'll get pie in the sky when you die.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And the starvation army they play,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And they sing and they clap and they pray.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Till they get all your coin on the drum,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Then they'll tell you when you're on the bum:</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Holy Rollers and jumpers come out,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And they holler, they jump and they shout.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>&quot;Give your money to Jesus,&quot; they say,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>&quot;He will cure all diseases today.&quot;</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>If you fight hard for children and wife—</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Try to get something good in this life—</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>You're a sinner and bad man, they tell,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>When you die you will sure go to hell.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Workingmen of all countries, unite,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Side by side we for freedom will fight:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>When the world and its wealth we have gained</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>To the grafters we'll sing this refrain:</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>You will eat, bye and bye,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>When you've learned how to cook and to fry</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Chop some wood, 'twill do you good,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And you'll eat in the sweet bye and bye.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Joe Hill (1911), from the IWW's Songs of the Workers, 15th edition, as on English Wikisource. Text from Wikisource (<a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Songs_of_the_Workers_(15th_edition)%2FThe_Preacher_and_the_Slave" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Songs of the Workers (15th edition)/The Preacher and the Slave</a>); public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
